{
  "id": "3ikq6yu",
  "title": "PHYSICS – CHAPTER 4",
  "order": 36,
  "blocks": [
    {
      "id": "h5kjorg",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "GRAVITATION"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "The Universal Law of Gravitation states that gravitational force is inversely proportional to the square of the distance between two masses.",
        "Mass is scalar and remains constant everywhere, whereas Weight is a vector quantity that varies with local acceleration due to gravity (g).",
        "Archimedes' Principle states that the upward buoyant force equals the weight of the fluid displaced by a submerged body.",
        "Kepler's Third Law specifies that the square of orbital period (T²) is proportional to the cube of the semi-major axis (r³).",
        "Escape velocity is independent of the mass of the escaping body and equals √2 times the orbital velocity."
      ],
      "id": "eblfmbd"
    },
    {
      "type": "statStrip",
      "stats": [
        {
          "label": "Avg Earth 'g'",
          "value": "9.8 m/s²",
          "icon": "check"
        },
        {
          "label": "Earth Escape Velocity",
          "value": "11.2 km/s",
          "icon": "award"
        },
        {
          "label": "Gravitational Constant (G)",
          "value": "6.67 × 10⁻¹¹ N m²/kg²",
          "icon": "info"
        },
        {
          "label": "Geostationary Period",
          "value": "24 Hours",
          "icon": "clock"
        }
      ],
      "id": "53c4ieg"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "6pouchx",
      "content": "Gravitation is the force of attraction between any two masses in the universe. Every object attracts every other object with a force known as gravitational force."
    },
    {
      "id": "xa7wa74",
      "type": "image",
      "imageId": "zsqcak5",
      "src": "/books/Precis/RRB GS/images/image_zsqcak5.png"
    },
    {
      "id": "ixkw1qs",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "1. UNIVERSAL LAW OF GRAVITATION"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "pj89aan",
      "content": "According to Newton’s Universal Law of Gravitation: Every two particles in the universe attract each other with a force directly proportional to the product of their masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between them."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ice7uu2",
      "content": "Mathematical Form:"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "vv2vvwr",
      "content": "F = G (m1 × m2) / r²"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "jrgmqyl",
      "content": "Where:"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "52o4o74",
      "content": "m1, m2 = masses"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "35vflmk",
      "content": "r = distance between centres"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "k0g18q9",
      "content": "G = Universal Gravitational Constant"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "6pjdemu",
      "content": "Value of G = 6.67 × 10⁻¹¹ N m²/kg²"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "lhwn33i",
      "content": "Dimension of G = [M⁻¹L³T⁻²]"
    },
    {
      "id": "ke5zdqj",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "2. ACCELERATION DUE TO GRAVITY (g)"
    },
    {
      "id": "auumz4q",
      "type": "image",
      "imageId": "qit7jlw",
      "src": "/books/Precis/RRB GS/images/image_qit7jlw.png"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "cow042m",
      "content": "g = GM / R²<img></img>"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "i0n3lh7",
      "content": "Value near Earth’s surface ≈ 9.8 m/s²"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "fjtqgd4",
      "content": "Depends on: Mass and Radius of Earth "
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "5602n4f",
      "content": "Variation of g:"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "mwhr60j",
      "content": "• g decreases with altitude"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "4iz45lu",
      "content": "• g decreases with depth"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "4igv0fi",
      "content": "• g is maximum at poles"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "vx8fg1j",
      "content": "• g is minimum at equator"
    },
    {
      "id": "ent3uh5",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "3. MASS VS WEIGHT"
    },
    {
      "id": "6nyn35w",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Feature</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Mass</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Weight</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Definition</p>",
            "<p>Amount of matter</p>",
            "<p>Force due to gravity</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Formula</p>",
            "<p>m</p>",
            "<p>W = mg</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>SI Unit</p>",
            "<p>Kilogram (kg)</p>",
            "<p>Newton (N)</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Nature</p>",
            "<p>Scalar</p>",
            "<p>Vector</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Variation</p>",
            "<p>Constant</p>",
            "<p>Varies with g</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "bodzwre",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "4. FREE FALL"
    },
    {
      "id": "ikc6bmm",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": ""
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "x34zrns",
      "content": "When a body falls under gravity alone, it is said to be in free fall."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "lu1rzbt",
      "content": "Equations:"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "lopx6m0",
      "content": "v = gt"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "0mu06ij",
      "content": "s = ½ gt²"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "gt3oh6k",
      "content": "v² = 2gh"
    },
    {
      "id": "m28mr0c",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "5. THRUST AND PRESSURE"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "tps5rwd",
      "content": "Thrust = Force acting perpendicular to surface."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "a28baff",
      "content": "Pressure = Force / Area"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ih4zvck",
      "content": "SI Unit of Pressure = Pascal (Pa)"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "05059n0",
      "content": "1 Pa = 1 N/m²"
    },
    {
      "id": "yj2a42s",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "6. BUOYANT FORCE"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "breaf6g",
      "content": "Buoyant force is the upward force exerted by a fluid on a body immersed in it."
    },
    {
      "id": "f02q7c8",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "7. ARCHIMEDES’ PRINCIPLE"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "67wxpwa",
      "content": "When a body is immersed in a fluid, it experiences an upward force equal to the weight of fluid displaced by it."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "c6dgso1",
      "content": "Buoyant Force = Weight of displaced fluid"
    },
    {
      "id": "5xfpg5n",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "8. RELATIVE DENSITY"
    },
    {
      "id": "gxptuf8",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Substance</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Relative Density</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Water</p>",
            "<p>1</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Iron</p>",
            "<p>7.8</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Mercury</p>",
            "<p>13.6</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "e5wzlob",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "9. FLOATATION"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ukqjj78",
      "content": "A body floats if its density is less than density of liquid."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "v230t6x",
      "content": "Weight of floating body = Weight of displaced liquid"
    },
    {
      "id": "t56sl4z",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "10. ESCAPE VELOCITY"
    },
    {
      "id": "8edrfk9",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": ""
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "3ievrls",
      "content": "Escape Velocity = √(2GM / R)"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "26dvyll",
      "content": "For Earth ≈ 11.2 km/s<img></img>"
    },
    {
      "id": "vftl3hb",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "11. SATELLITE MOTION"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "nfn82lw",
      "content": "Orbital Velocity = √(GM / R)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "nwigzzg",
      "content": "Orbital Velocity of Earth ≈ 7.9 km/s"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "b6agaez",
      "content": "Time Period of satellite: T² ∝ R³ (Kepler’s Third Law)"
    },
    {
      "id": "a08lp4c",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "12. RELATION BETWEEN g AND G"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "i4h894a",
      "content": "We know: g = GM / R²"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "lfrgs69",
      "content": "Where M = Mass of Earth, R = Radius of Earth"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "y1y3s9y",
      "content": "Using this formula, we can derive mass or density of Earth."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "z50imf7",
      "content": "Density of Earth (ρ) = 3g / (4πGR)"
    },
    {
      "id": "szy3pzv",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "13. VARIATION OF g WITH HEIGHT AND DEPTH"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "h45aatj",
      "content": "At height h above Earth’s surface:"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ej3vek0",
      "content": "g_h = g (R / (R + h))²"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "sggduad",
      "content": "As height increases, value of g decreases."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "r16c05g",
      "content": "At depth d below Earth’s surface:"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "k3f5y5s",
      "content": "g_d = g (1 - d/R)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "6nmdqkj",
      "content": "g becomes zero at centre of Earth."
    },
    {
      "id": "4w01620",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "14. GRAVITATIONAL POTENTIAL ENERGY"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "lnmwqgm",
      "content": "Near Earth’s surface: PE = mgh"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "hgiu30x",
      "content": "General form: PE = - GMm / r"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "21hwexi",
      "content": "Negative sign indicates attractive nature of gravitational force."
    },
    {
      "id": "z8g7u43",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "15. ESCAPE VELOCITY – ADVANCED POINTS"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "zxbyic2",
      "content": "Escape Velocity = √(2GM / R)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "aval0sz",
      "content": "Relation: Escape Velocity = √2 × Orbital Velocity"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "mnkudbf",
      "content": "Does not depend on mass of object."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "spily2j",
      "content": "For Earth ≈ 11.2 km/s"
    },
    {
      "id": "cp8b6v2",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "16. ORBITAL VELOCITY & TIME PERIOD"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "3a6315a",
      "content": "Orbital Velocity = √(GM / R)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "cog7f9v",
      "content": "Time Period of Satellite: T = 2πR / v"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "hhf1vk9",
      "content": "Kepler’s Third Law: T² ∝ R³"
    },
    {
      "id": "lw5wmol",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "17. KEPLER’S LAWS OF PLANETARY MOTION"
    },
    {
      "id": "yfntv3q",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Law</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Statement</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>First Law</p>",
            "<p>Planets move in elliptical orbits with Sun at one focus</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Second Law</p>",
            "<p>Equal areas in equal intervals of time</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Third Law</p>",
            "<p>Square of time period ∝ Cube of orbital radius</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "sw4vbl4",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "18. GEOSTATIONARY SATELLITE"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "l9dziye",
      "content": "Conditions:"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "s93v1rr",
      "content": "• Time period = 24 hours"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "j9vqu1k",
      "content": "• Moves in equatorial plane"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ymkqp6c",
      "content": "• Moves from west to east"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "qk8ai2k",
      "content": "Height ≈ 36,000 km above Earth surface"
    },
    {
      "id": "jdoiz0y",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "19. WEIGHTLESSNESS"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "5094p6m",
      "content": "Weightlessness occurs when normal reaction becomes zero."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "p9fq648",
      "content": "Example: Astronauts inside orbiting satellite."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "5seirur",
      "content": "Cause: Continuous free fall condition."
    },
    {
      "id": "mlbnpcd",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "20. TERMINAL VELOCITY (BASIC CONCEPT)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "azv1zzl",
      "content": "When gravitational force equals air resistance, body falls with constant velocity called terminal velocity."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "5bwpvlb",
      "content": "<strong>21.ADVANCED RRB EXAM POINTS</strong>"
    },
    {
      "id": "dlgenrs",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Escape Velocity = √2 × Orbital Velocity."
    },
    {
